            "url": "https://builtin.com/company/federal-reserve-bank-chicago/faq/workplace-perception",
            "text": "[[Built In Logo] [Built In Logo]][1][Companies][2][Jobs][3][Articles][4]\n[View All Jobs][5]\n[ Join ][6]\n[ Log In ][7]\n[ For Employers ][8]\n[ Companies ][9] [ Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago ][10]\n\n## Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\n\nSave Saved\nChicago\nTotal Offices: **2**\n1,515 Total Employees\nYear Founded: 1914\n[View Website][11]\nWe're Hiring!\n[ Cybersecurity **1** ][12]\n[View all jobs][13]\n* Overview\n* Jobs 1\n* Offices\n* Articles\n* Company Insights\n* [ Working at Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago ][14]\n* [ Culture & Values ][15]\n* [ Career Growth & Development ][16]\n* [ Compensation & Benefits ][17]\n* [ Work-Life Balance & Wellbeing ][18]\n* [ Leadership & Management ][19]\n* [ Innovation, Technology & Agility ][20]\n* [ Stability & Growth ][21]\n\n# What's It Like to Work at Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago?\n\nUpdated on June 04, 2026\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n## What practices at Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago support employee job satisfaction?\n\nJob Satisfaction\n\nFederal Reserve Bank of Chicago supports employee job satisfaction by offering meaningful work tied\nto its public-service mission, along with opportunities for growth and a supportive workplace\nculture. Employees often highlight the combination of impactful work, strong colleagues and\nprofessional development as key reasons they enjoy working at the Bank.\n* **Mission-driven work with public impact:** The Chicago Fed\u2019s work is rooted in serving the public\ninterest by fostering a strong economy and promoting financial stability. Employees connect that\nmission to their day-to-day roles, from bank supervision and economic research to technology,\nfacilities and financial services. A supervision manager said he is proud to contribute to \u201ca\nmission that centers on contributing to the stability of the financial system, nationally and\nglobally.\u201d\n* **Growth through learning and new opportunities: **The Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago emphasizes\ncareer development through varied projects, learning opportunities across teams and the Federal\nReserve System, and pathways to pursue new challenges. Employees describe opportunities to expand\ntheir skills, take on new responsibilities and explore different career paths within the\norganization. A business solutions analyst said he has taken on \u201cnew and exciting roles\u201d\nthroughout his Fed career, a senior business analyst described \u201cendless opportunities,\u201d and a\nsenior risk specialist said the Chicago Fed \u201ctruly values constant growth and learning.\u201d\n* **Collaborative, supportive culture: **Employees frequently point to strong relationships,\nmentorship and cross-functional teamwork as reasons they enjoy working at the Bank. A vice\npresident described \u201ctalented, thoughtful and giving individuals who do great work that makes a\ndifference,\u201d while a research analyst cited mentorship from economists and an \u201cintellectual and\nsupportive culture.\u201d The Bank also emphasizes collaboration, inclusion, psychological safety and\nemployee communities that help foster connection and professional growth.\n* **Benefits and work-life support:** The Chicago Fed\u2019s benefits experience is designed to help\nemployees focus on their work, pursue long-term careers and manage responsibilities outside the\noffice. Health coverage, retirement programs, paid leave, family-support resources, educational\nassistance and wellness offerings provide practical support that can reduce stress and help\nemployees balance professional and personal commitments. These resources complement the Bank\u2019s\nemphasis on learning, career growth and employee well-being, while external reviews frequently\ncite benefits and work-life balance as positive aspects of the overall employee experience.\n* **External signals:**\n* **Work environment and culture:** Employees on external review sites frequently mention a\npositive work environment, meaningful work, supportive colleagues and a strong sense of mission.\nExternal ratings also indicate positive sentiment toward leadership, including a reported 76%\nCEO approval rating. (Glassdoor; Indeed)\n* **Flexibility, learning and work-life balance:** Employees on external review sites also\nhighlight flexibility, learning opportunities and work-life balance as strengths of the employee\nexperience. This is reflected in its work-life balance external rating score of 3.7/5.\n(Glassdoor; Indeed)\n* **Supportive benefits: **Employees on external review sites frequently cite benefits as a\npositive aspect of working at the Bank. External ratings include a reported 4.0/5 compensation\nand benefits score, reflecting favorable perceptions of the Bank\u2019s rewards offerings.\n(Glassdoor)\n\n**Bottom line:** Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago supports job satisfaction by combining purposeful\npublic-service work, long-term career development, collaborative teams and comprehensive benefits\nthat help employees grow while contributing to a mission with broad economic impact.\n\n## Is Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago a good place to work? Do Employees Recommend it?\n\nWillingness to Recommend\n\nYes, Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago is a strong workplace for employees who value mission-driven\nwork, steady career development, collaborative colleagues, comprehensive benefits and the\nopportunity to contribute to the stability of the economy and financial system. Employees describe a\nworkplace where people can do meaningful work while continuing to learn, build relationships and\ngrow across the organization. (Chicago Fed Careers; Built In; Glassdoor; Indeed)\n* **Purpose across many career paths: **Employees at the Chicago Fed contribute across technology,\nresearch, bank supervision, professional services, financial products and services, and law\nenforcement. The Bank describes its work as serving the public interest through economic research,\nbank supervision, community outreach, education and central bank services. That broad mission\ngives employees different ways to connect their roles to public impact and the greater good.\n* **Employees recommend the growth environment: **Employees highlight opportunities for learning,\nmobility and new challenges as reasons to work at the Bank. A technology leader said opportunities\nto \u201cgrow and take on new challenges\u201d make the Chicago Fed a good place to work, while a cash\nsupervisor said the Federal Reserve provides \u201cendless opportunities\u201d through varied work that\nsupports career development.\n* **A culture built around people and collaboration:** Employees also recommend the Chicago Fed\nbecause of its colleagues and team environment. One employee described the Bank as a place with\n\u201ctalented, thoughtful and giving individuals,\u201d while another said successful employees value\ncollaboration, self-awareness, relationships and the bigger picture. This aligns with the Bank\u2019s\nvalues of integrity, respect, responsibility and excellence.\n* **Benefits that support long-term careers: **Employees often point to the Chicago Fed\u2019s benefits\nas part of its appeal as a long-term employer. Rather than focusing on individual perks, the\noverall package is designed to support employees\u2019 financial security, health, professional\ndevelopment and family needs throughout different stages of their careers. Examples include a\nfully employer-funded pension, retirement savings support, tuition reimbursement and\nfamily-focused resources.\n* **External signals:**\n* **Workplace strengths: **Glassdoor and Indeed reviews point to meaningful work, supportive\ncolleagues, work-life balance, learning opportunities, strong benefits and a positive work\nenvironment. (Glassdoor; Indeed)\n* **Recommendation signals:** Employees on external review sites rate the CEO highly, giving it a\n76% approval rating, while review excerpts describe the Chicago Fed as a \u201cgreat place to work,\u201d\na \u201cgreat place to learn\u201d and an \u201cawesome place to work.\u201d (Glassdoor)\n* **Benefits and balance:** External reviews frequently cite benefits and work-life balance as\nstrengths, including a 4.0/5 compensation and benefits rating and 3.7/5 work-life balance rating\non Glassdoor. (Glassdoor; Indeed)\n\n**Bottom line: **Employees recommend Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago for its combination of\nmission-oriented work, collaborative culture, career development opportunities and benefits that\nsupport both professional growth and life outside work.\n\n## Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago's Candidate Tradeoffs\n\n**If you\u2019re weighing whether Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago is the right fit, these are the core\ntradeoffs to consider.**\n* Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago emphasizes a process-driven organization designed to deliver\nconsistent, reliable results, though that reflects a disciplined approach to planning and\nstructured execution.\n\n## Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago Employee Perspectives\n\nFederal Reserve Bank of Chicago offers a workplace where intellectually curious employees can\nconnect their work to a broader public-service mission. Team members describe the Bank as a place\nthat values meaningful impact, work-life balance, diversity of thought, and inclusion.\n\n\u201cChicago Fed is a satisfying place to work for someone who seeks an intellectually stimulating\nenvironment that has a mission geared toward serving consumers and the safety and soundness of the\nbanking system. It\u2019s also an environment for someone who values work-life balance and a place that\nis committed to diversity of thought and inclusion of all individuals.\u201d\n\nGustavo, Supervision Manager, Supervision & Regulation, Bank Supervision\nFrom the article: [Meet Gustavo][22]\n\n## Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago Employee Reviews\n\nOver the past several years I have come across some very passionate, motivated and intelligent\npeople. It has been a pleasure to work and collaborate with all of these individuals and also\nestablish strong friendships along the way.\n\nRicky, Assistant VP of Information Technology\nRicky, Assistant VP of Information Technology\n\nI recommend the Chicago Fed Research Assistant Program to anyone who is curious in pursuing a career\nin economic research. At the Chicago Fed, RAs have opportunity to conduct research, learn about\nmonetary policy, and acquire new technical skills.\n\nMay, Research Analyst, Economic Research\nMay, Research Analyst, Economic Research\n\nThe Bank\u2019s values of respect, responsibility, integrity and operational excellence resonate with me.\nI am proud to be, in some small way, a part of a mission that centers on contributing to the\nstability of the financial system, nationally and globally.\n\nGustavo, Supervision Manager, Supervision & Regulation\nGustavo, Supervision Manager, Supervision & Regulation\n\n## What People Are Saying About Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\n* Mission & Purpose: Day-to-day work centers on monetary policy, bank supervision, payments, and\nregional economic analysis, creating a direct connection to financial stability and the public\ninterest. This clear sense of purpose is described as motivating and meaningful.\n* Benefits & Perks: The organization highlights comprehensive health coverage, generous time off,\ndisability coverage, family-building support, and an additional employer\u2011funded retirement benefit\nalongside other savings options. Flexible schedules and telework options are also noted where\nrole-appropriate.\n* Learning & Development: Career materials emphasize development, mobility across teams, and\ncollaboration across the Federal Reserve System. Exposure to varied projects and cross\u2011team\nlearning is positioned as a core part of the experience.\n\n## Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago's Benefits\n\n### Promotion Policies\n\nEncourages lateral mobility to expand skills and impact\n\nPosts new positions internally and encourages employees to apply\n\nPromote from within\n\n### Work Environment\n\nDefined policies promoting a professional, respectful workplace\n\nDefined values and mission statements\n\nOpen office floor plan to encourage communication and collaboration\n\nPolicies promote a low-ego, team-driven culture\n\nPrioritizes mission-driven work in decision-making processes\n\nPrioritizes real-world i"
