            "url": "https://builtin.com/company/gitlab/faq/workplace-perception",
            "text": "[[Built In Logo] [Built In Logo]][1][Companies][2][Jobs][3][Articles][4]\n[View All Jobs][5]\n[ Join ][6]\n[ Log In ][7]\n[ For Employers ][8]\n[ Companies ][9] [ GitLab ][10]\n\n## GitLab\n\nSave Saved\nSan Francisco\n2,500 Total Employees\nYear Founded: 2014\n[View Website][11]\nWe're Hiring!\n[ Sales **48** ][12] [ Engineering **40** ][13] [ Customer Success & Experience **12** ][14] [\nOperations & Support **7** ][15] [ Consulting **4** ][16] [ Cybersecurity **3** ][17] [ AI & Machine\nLearning **3** ][18] [ HR & Recruiting **3** ][19] [ Legal **3** ][20] [ Marketing **3** ][21] [\nProduct Management **3** ][22] [ Design **2** ][23] [ Finance **2** ][24] [ Data & Analytics **1**\n][25] [ Program & Project Management **1** ][26]\n[View all jobs][27]\n* Overview\n* Jobs 135\n* Offices\n* Teams\n* Culture\n* Articles\n* Company Insights\n* [ Working at GitLab ][28]\n* [ Culture & Values ][29]\n* [ Inclusion & Diversity ][30]\n* [ Career Growth & Development ][31]\n* [ Compensation & Benefits ][32]\n* [ Work-Life Balance & Wellbeing ][33]\n* [ Leadership & Management ][34]\n* [ Innovation, Technology & Agility ][35]\n* [ Mission, Purpose & Impact ][36]\n* [ Stability & Growth ][37]\n\n# What's It Like to Work at GitLab?\n\nUpdated on May 29, 2026\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n## What practices at GitLab support employee job satisfaction?\n\nJob Satisfaction\n\nAt GitLab, job satisfaction is driven by a combination of autonomy, flexibility, and a deeply\nembedded culture of transparency and collaboration. GitLab empowers team members to contribute,\ngrow, and thrive. Employees are empowered as \u201cmanagers of one,\u201d taking ownership of their goals and\nwork, while benefiting from a culture that encourages iteration, continuous feedback, and open\ncontribution across the organization. Leadership reinforces this environment through direct\ncommunication, regular feedback loops, and a strong emphasis on trust, accountability, and\nself-leadership.\n\nGitLab also supports employee well-being and growth through comprehensive benefits and development\nopportunities. Team members have access to flexible PTO, mental health support, parental leave, and\na remote-first work environment that allows them to be present for personal priorities. Professional\ndevelopment is supported through learning stipends, mentorship, conferences, and internal mobility\nopportunities, while equity compensation ensures employees share in the company\u2019s long-term success.\nInclusive practices such as Team Member Resource Groups (TMRGs) and a culture of excellence further\nreinforce a sense of belonging and purpose, helping employees feel supported both personally and\nprofessionally.\n\n**Employee Perspective**\n\n\u201cGitLab has been tremendously helpful in navigating these responsibilities. I can be present for\nimportant moments while still delivering excellent results for my clients and team.\u201d\n\n\u2014 Shannon Maccado, Strategic Account Executive\n\n**At-a-Glance**\n* **Core benefits:** Flexible PTO, remote work support, mental health benefits, parental leave,\nequity and ESPP, learning stipends\n* **Feedback rhythm:** Continuous feedback culture with regular manager check-ins, async\ncollaboration, and documented workflows\n* **Survey insight:** Employees frequently cite flexibility, strong collaboration, growth\nopportunities, and supportive leadership as key motivators (employee perspectives)\n\n**External Signals**\n* **Employee recommendation sentiment:** Employees rate GitLab\u2019s culture highly, giving it an A+ on\nreview sites \u2014 with employees highlighting the culture of transparency and learning opportunities\n\u2014 earning it a status as a \u201cChoice Employer.\u201d (Comparably 2026)\n* **External validation:** Great Place to Work\u2013certified (2026) with 92% of employees rating it as a\n\u201cgreat place to work\u201d (Great Place to Work).\n\n## Is GitLab a good place to work? Do Employees Recommend it?\n\nWillingness to Recommend\n\nGitLab is widely regarded as a strong place to work, particularly for those who value flexibility,\nautonomy, and a transparent, values-driven culture. Employees are empowered as \u201cmanagers of one,\u201d\ngiving them ownership over their work and career progression.\n\nInternally, employees consistently highlight strong support systems, meaningful work, and access to\nleadership as key reasons they recommend GitLab. The company invests heavily in career growth\nthrough internal mobility, mentorship, and a $10,000 annual Growth and Development Fund, while also\nsupporting well-being with flexible PTO, mental health resources, and a fully remote work\nenvironment that enables work-life balance. Inclusive practices such as Team Member Resource Groups\n(TMRGs) and a globally distributed workforce reinforce a sense of belonging and shared purpose.\nWhile GitLab maintains a high-performance culture with an emphasis on accountability and results,\nthis is balanced by strong collaboration, transparency, and a culture of feedback that helps\nemployees succeed.\n\nExternal signals and employee perspectives further reinforce GitLab\u2019s positive reputation. The\ncompany is Great Place to Work\u2013certified and frequently recognized for its remote-first model and\ninnovation culture. Employees often describe the company as empowering, collaborative, and\ngrowth-oriented, with many emphasizing that they feel valued and supported in their roles. At the\nsame time, GitLab is transparent about its expectations: employees are expected to be self-directed,\ncomfortable with async work, and motivated by outcomes, which may not suit every working style.\n\n**At-a-Glance**\n* **Positive environment: **High-trust, remote culture with strong collaboration and transparency.\n* **Goal alignment: **Clear focus on results, ownership, and customer impact through values-driven\nwork.\n\n**External Signals**\n* **Employee recommendation sentiment: **Employees rate GitLab\u2019s culture highly giving it an A+ on\nreview sites \u2014 with employees highlighting the culture of transparency, remote work, and learning\nopportunities \u2014 earning it a status as a \u201cChoice Employer.\u201d (Comparably 2026)\n* **External validation: **Great Place to Work\u2013certified (2026) with 92% of employees rating it as a\n\u201cgreat place to work\u201d (Great Place to Work).\n\n## What reputation does GitLab have as a place to work?\n\nGeneral Market Perception\n\nGitLab has built a strong reputation as a forward-thinking company known for its transparency,\nautonomy, and a culture built on speed with quality, ownership mindset and customer outcomes.\nGitlab\u2019s open-source roots and public handbook reinforce a reputation for transparency, where\ncompany processes, decisions, and even strategy are visible and accessible. This openness, combined\nwith a culture that emphasizes iteration, collaboration, and efficiency, positions GitLab as an\ninnovative and modern workplace in the technology industry.\n\nEmployee sentiment further reinforces this reputation, with many highlighting the company\u2019s\nsupportive environment, strong leadership access, and opportunities for growth. Team members\nfrequently describe feeling empowered to contribute, supported by managers who act as coaches and\nadvocates, and part of a collaborative, global community. GitLab\u2019s emphasis on internal mobility,\ncontinuous learning, and ownership culture\u2014where individuals are trusted to manage their own\nwork\u2014contributes to a perception of the company as a place where careers can grow quickly and\nmeaningfully. At the same time, GitLab\u2019s high-performance, results-oriented environment is clearly\ncommunicated, shaping a reputation as a place best suited for self-directed, motivated individuals.\n\nExternally, GitLab is recognized for its innovation in DevSecOps, its leadership in remote work, and\nits inclusive, globally distributed workforce spanning 65+ countries. Its Great Place to Work\ncertification and consistent employee testimonials highlighting flexibility, transparency, and\ncollaboration further strengthen its employer brand. The company is often associated with\ncutting-edge technology development, particularly in AI and software delivery, reinforcing its\nreputation as both a technically advanced and culturally progressive organization.\n\n**Employee Perspective**\n\n\u201cWhat really struck me early on was how GitLab genuinely cares about its employees as whole people,\nnot just as resources. You\u2019re not just another number here \u2014 there\u2019s real investment in your success\nand well-being.\u201d\n\n**\u2014 Shannon Maccado, Strategic Account Executive**\n\n**At-a-Glance**\n* **Overall reputation: **Innovative, transparent, and remote-first leader in the tech industry\n* **Culture & leadership: **Values-driven, high-trust environment with strong leadership access and\ncoaching\n* **Employee sentiment: **Empowering, collaborative, and growth-oriented, with high ownership and\nautonomy\n* **Employer brand signals: **Great Place to Work\u2013certified; recognized for remote work leadership\nand DevSecOps innovation\n\n**External Signals**\n* **Employee recommendation sentiment: **Employees rate GitLab\u2019s culture highly, giving it an A+ on\nreview sites \u2014 with employees highlighting the culture of transparency, remote work, and learning\nopportunities \u2014 earning it a status as a \u201cChoice Employer.\u201d (Comparably 2026)\n* **External validation: **Great Place to Work\u2013certified (2026) with 92% of employees rating it as a\n\u201cgreat place to work\u201d (Great Place to Work).\n\n## What tradeoffs might come with working at GitLab?\n\nTradeoffs\n\nGitLab emphasizes autonomy with accountability and end-to-end ownership, empowering employees to\ntake initiative, make decisions, and own outcomes. While this creates meaningful impact and faster\ndecision-making, it can come with less day-to-day guidance and a higher expectation for individuals\nto proactively seek information, manage priorities, and drive their own work forward.\n\nAnother key tradeoff comes from GitLab\u2019s experimentation-led, iteration-driven culture. The company\nprioritizes rapid learning, continuous improvement, and shipping incremental updates, which fuels\ninnovation and growth. At the same time, this can lead to frequent changes, evolving priorities, and\na level of complexity that requires adaptability. According to GitLab\u2019s AI reputation report, this\ndynamic contributes to a \u201ccautiously positive\u201d perception\u2014highlighting strengths like flexibility\nand transparency alongside challenges such as change fatigue and coordination overhead.\n\nEmployees often describe this balance as both challenging and rewarding. The same systems that\nrequire independence and adaptability\u2014such as async workflows and a handbook-first approach\u2014also\nenable visibility, learning, and career growth.\n\nLeadership works to balance these tradeoffs through transparent documentation, clear goal-setting\nframeworks like OKRs, and structured processes such as promotion cycles and annual compensation\nreviews. Wellbeing and sustainability are reinforced through flexible PTO, mental health support,\nand a culture that encourages boundary-setting and prioritizing personal needs.\n\nThe tradeoffs are offset by major strengths, including strong alignment to GitLab\u2019s values\n(transparency, iteration, and collaboration), meaningful ownership of work, and the ability to\ncollaborate globally in a high-impact environment. External signals reinforce this balance, with\nGitLab earning a 76% AI reputation score and being described as \u201ccautiously positive,\u201d reflecting\nboth its strengths and areas of complexity.\n\n**Great match for candidates who prefer:**\n* Autonomy with accountability and ownership of outcomes\n* Fast-paced, iteration-driven environments\n* Transparent leadership and documentation-driven workflows\n\n**Working here means:**\n* Taking initiative in a self-directed work environment\n* Navigating evolving priorities and continuous iteration\n* Owning results, not just responsibilities\n\n**At-a-Glance**\n* **Autonomy: **High ownership with decentralized decision-making\n* **Pace:** Iteration-driven with frequent updates and evolving priorities\n* **Support systems: **Flexible PTO, mental health resources, structured per"
          },
