            "text": "[ Skip to main content ][1]\n[U.S. flag]\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere\u2019s how you know\n\nHere\u2019s how you know\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\nA **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\nA **lock** ( [Lock] ) or **https://** means you\u2019ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share\nsensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n* [Our Purpose][2]\n* [Our Science and Technology][3]\n* [Join Our Team][4]\n* [Partner with Us][5]\n* [News][6]\n* [Community and Education][7]\n* [About][8]\n\n[[LLNL Logo]][9]\n[National Ignition Facility & Photon Science][10]\n\n## Primary Menu\n* [About ][11]\n* [What Is NIF?][12]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][13]\n* [Laser Glass][14]\n* [Optical Switch][15]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][16]\n* [Deformable Mirror][17]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][18]\n* [Computer Control System][19]\n* [Target Fabrication][20]\n* [How NIF Works][21]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][22]\n* [Power Conditioning System][23]\n* [Injection Laser System][24]\n* [Amplifiers][25]\n* [Beam Transport][26]\n* [Final Optics][27]\n* [Target Chamber][28]\n* [Keys to Our Success][29]\n* [Facility Operations][30]\n* [Power and Energy][31]\n* [Control Systems][32]\n* [Optics][33]\n* [Targets][34]\n* [Diagnostics][35]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][36]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][37]\n* [Who Works for NIF][38]\n* [People Profiles][39]\n* [Management][40]\n* [Developing the Workforce][41]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][42]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][43]\n* [Technology Transfer][44]\n* [Careers][45]\n* [FAQs][46]\n* [Visit Us][47]\n* [Science ][48]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][49]\n* [High Energy Density Science][50]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][51]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][52]\n* [National Security Applications][53]\n* [Developing New Capabilities][54]\n* [Discovery Science][55]\n* [Lab Astrophysics][56]\n* [Nuclear Astrophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [Laser Glass][128]\n* [Optical Switch][129]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][130]\n* [Deformable Mirror][131]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][132]\n* [Computer Control System][133]\n* [Target Fabrication][134]\n* [How NIF Works][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [Laser Glass][159]\n* [Optical Switch][160]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][161]\n* [Deformable Mirror][162]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][163]\n* [Computer Control System][164]\n* [Target Fabrication][165]\n* [How NIF Works][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. [The Seven Wonders of NIF][175]\n4. Laser Glass\n\n# Laser Glass\n\n[Technician Examines Continuous-Pour Laser Glass]\nThe fabrication of melted and rough-cut blanks of laser glass amplifier slabs needed for NIF\u2019s\nconstruction (3,072 pieces) was completed in 2005. The amplifier slabs are neodymium-doped phosphate\nglass manufactured by Hoya Corp. USA and SCHOTT North America.\n\nLaser glass is the heart of the NIF laser system; it\u2019s the material that amplifies the laser light\nto the very high energies required for experiments.\n\nNIF\u2019s laser glass is a phosphate glass that contains neodymium atoms (Nd:glass). Neodymium-doped\nlaser glass is the preferred gain medium for use in high-peak-power lasers for fusion energy\nresearch.\n\nThe NIF laser system uses about 3,070 42-kilogram plates of laser glass. Each glass plate measures\n3.4 by 46 by 81 centimeters (about 3 feet long and about half as wide). If stacked end-to-end, the\nplates would form a continuous ribbon of glass 1.5 miles long. The glass slabs are set on edge at a\nspecific angle, known as Brewster\u2019s angle, so that the laser beams have very low reflective losses\nwhile propagating through the glass.\n\n[A NIF Outer Flashlamp Cassette]\nA NIF outer flashlamp cassette.\n\nTo pros glass quickly enough to meet construction schedules, Hoya Corporation, USA, and SCHOTT North\nAmerica developed a new production process that melts raw materials into one continuously flowing\nstrip of high optical-quality laser glass. Once cooled, the glass is cut into pieces as it leaves\nthe production system; the segments are then polished to the demanding NIF specifications. This\nnovel, continuous melting process makes meter-sized plates of laser glass at a rate 20 times faster,\n5 times cheaper, and with 2 to 3 times better optical quality than with previous processes.\n\n## Flashlamps\n\nEach NIF beamline contains two large amplifier sections designed to efficiently amplify the 1-joule\ninput pulse from the injection laser system to each of the 192 beams to the required power and\nenergy while maintaining high beam quality. The amplifiers, with 16 glass slabs per beam, are\narranged in two amplifier sections\u2014the main amplifier and the power amplifier. Together, these\namplifiers provide 99.99 percent of NIF\u2019s energy and power.\n\nThe amplifier slabs are surrounded by vertical arrays of flashlamps. Measuring nearly 180\ncentimeters (6 feet) of arc length, NIF\u2019s 7,680 flashlamps are the largest commercial units ever\nmade. Each is driven with about 50,000 joules of electrical energy. The flashlamps excite the\nneodymium in the glass slabs to provide optical gain at the infrared frequency of 1,053-nanometer\nwavelength, also referred to as \u201cone omega,\u201d or 1\u03c9, light. Some of the energy stored in the\nneodymium is released when the laser pulses from the injection laser system pass through the\namplifier slabs (see [How Lasers Work)][176].\n\nNext Up: [Optical Switch][177]\n\n[[LLNL]][178]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-642493\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][179]\n[[LLNS]][180] [[U.S. DOE]][181] [[NNSA]][182]\n[Home][183] [Disclaimer][184] [Privacy Notice][185]\n* [About][186]\n* [What Is NIF?][187]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][188]\n* [Laser Glass][189]\n* [Optical Switch][190]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][191]\n* [Deformable Mirror][192]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][193]\n* [Computer Control System][194]\n* [Target Fabrication][195]\n* [How NIF Works][196]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][197]\n* [Power Conditioning System][198]\n* [Injection Laser System][199]\n* [Amplifiers][200]\n* [Beam Transport][201]\n* [Final Optics][202]\n* [Target Chamber][203]\n* [Keys to Our Success][204]\n* [Facility Operations][205]\n* [Power and Energy][206]\n* [Control Systems][207]\n* [Optics][208]\n* [Targets][209]\n* [Diagnostics][210]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][211]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][212]\n* [Key Steps on the Path to NIF][213]\n* [50 Years of Laser Leadership][214]\n* [Building NIF][215]\n* [Who Works for NIF][216]\n* [People Profiles][217]\n* [Management][218]\n* [Developing the Workforce][219]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][220]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][221]\n* [Technology Transfer][222]\n* [Careers][223]\n* [FAQs][224]\n* [Visit Us][225]\n* [Science][226]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][227]\n* [High Energy Density Science][228]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][229]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][230]\n* [National Security Applications][231]\n* [Developing New Capabilities][232]\n* [