rophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [Laser Glass][128]\n* [Optical Switch][129]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][130]\n* [Deformable Mirror][131]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][132]\n* [Computer Control System][133]\n* [Target Fabrication][134]\n* [How NIF Works][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [Laser Glass][159]\n* [Optical Switch][160]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][161]\n* [Deformable Mirror][162]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][163]\n* [Computer Control System][164]\n* [Target Fabrication][165]\n* [How NIF Works][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. [The Seven Wonders of NIF][175]\n4. Preamplifier Modules\n\n# Preamplifier Modules\n\n[Technicians Prepare a NIF Preamplifier Module]\nTechnicians assemble and align the components in a NIF preamplifier module.\n\nNIF\u2019s master oscillator generates a very small, low-energy laser pulse (see [Injection Laser\nSystem][176]). The pulse may range from less than 100 trillionths to 25 billionths of a second long\nand has a specific temporal shape as requested by NIF experimenters. The low-energy pulse is split\nand carried on optical fibers to 48 preamplifier modules (PAMs) for initial amplification and beam\nconditioning and shaping.\n\nThe PAMs increase the energy by a factor of 10 billion to about 10 joules. Each PAM is a line\nreplaceable unit containing two different amplification stages. The first is a regenerative\namplifier that amplifies the pulse energy from about 750 picojoules (trillionths of a joule) to\nabout 15 millijoules (mJ) (thousandths of a joule), an amplification factor of 20 million. The\ninjected Master Oscillator Room (MOR) pulses travel through a laser diode-pumped neodymium-doped\nglass rod 116 times. The pumped light derives from a 4-kilowatt array of laser diodes whose output\nis channeled into one end of the rod via a hollow-lens duct light concentrator. The output beam from\nthe amplifier is then shaped from a round 3-millimeter (mm) Gaussian beam to a square 18x18-mm beam.\n\nThe second amplification stage amplifies the shaped beam by a factor of up to 10,000 using a\nfour-pass relay imaging laser amplifier. The amplifier is a neodymium glass rod pumped by 12\nflashlamps. The output energy of the PAM can be varied from about 10 mJ to about 10 joules.\n\nPAMs perform three kinds of precision beam shaping:\n* Spatial shaping to make the square beam more intense around the edges to compensate for the higher\ngain profile in the center of the large amplifiers\n* Spectral shaping and beam smoothing to eliminate both hot spots and dark spots at the focus by\nmanipulating the focal beam pattern with fast changes in wavelengths\n* Temporal shaping to ensure that the laser pulse delivers energy to the target with a prescribed\ntime-history for controlled compression and ignition.\n\nThe 48 beams from the 48 PAMs are then split into four beams each for injection into the 192 main\nlaser amplifier beamlines.\n\nNext Up: [Deformable Mirror][177]\n\n[[LLNL]][178]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-642493\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][179]\n[[LLNS]][180] [[U.S. DOE]][181] [[NNSA]][182]