            "text": "[ Skip to main content ][1]\n[U.S. flag]\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere\u2019s how you know\n\nHere\u2019s how you know\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\nA **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\nA **lock** ( [Lock] ) or **https://** means you\u2019ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share\nsensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n* [Our Purpose][2]\n* [Our Science and Technology][3]\n* [Join Our Team][4]\n* [Partner with Us][5]\n* [News][6]\n* [Community and Education][7]\n* [About][8]\n\n[[LLNL Logo]][9]\n[National Ignition Facility & Photon Science][10]\n\n## Primary Menu\n* [About ][11]\n* [What Is NIF?][12]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][13]\n* [Laser Glass][14]\n* [Optical Switch][15]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][16]\n* [Deformable Mirror][17]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][18]\n* [Computer Control System][19]\n* [Target Fabrication][20]\n* [How NIF Works][21]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][22]\n* [Power Conditioning System][23]\n* [Injection Laser System][24]\n* [Amplifiers][25]\n* [Beam Transport][26]\n* [Final Optics][27]\n* [Target Chamber][28]\n* [Keys to Our Success][29]\n* [Facility Operations][30]\n* [Power and Energy][31]\n* [Control Systems][32]\n* [Optics][33]\n* [Targets][34]\n* [Diagnostics][35]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][36]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][37]\n* [Who Works for NIF][38]\n* [People Profiles][39]\n* [Management][40]\n* [Developing the Workforce][41]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][42]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][43]\n* [Technology Transfer][44]\n* [Careers][45]\n* [FAQs][46]\n* [Visit Us][47]\n* [Science ][48]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][49]\n* [High Energy Density Science][50]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][51]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][52]\n* [National Security Applications][53]\n* [Developing New Capabilities][54]\n* [Discovery Science][55]\n* [Lab Astrophysics][56]\n* [Nuclear Astrophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [Laser Glass][128]\n* [Optical Switch][129]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][130]\n* [Deformable Mirror][131]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][132]\n* [Computer Control System][133]\n* [Target Fabrication][134]\n* [How NIF Works][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [Laser Glass][159]\n* [Optical Switch][160]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][161]\n* [Deformable Mirror][162]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][163]\n* [Computer Control System][164]\n* [Target Fabrication][165]\n* [How NIF Works][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. [The Seven Wonders of NIF][175]\n4. Deformable Mirror\n\n# Deformable Mirror\n\n[Technician Tests Deformable Mirror]\nA technician performs wavefront validation testing on a NIF deformable mirror.\n\nThe deformable mirror is an adaptive optic that uses an array of actuators to bend its surface to\ncompensate for wavefront errors in the NIF laser beams. There is one deformable mirror for each of\nNIF\u2019s 192 beams. Each mirror is located at the end of the main amplifier.\n\nThe computer-controlled mirrors serve as the eyeglasses of NIF. Thirty-nine actuators are attached\nto the back of the mirror. These mirrors push or pull the optical surface to correct optical\ndistortion in the beam from residual thermal distortion, imperfect optical materials and surface\nflatness, and amplifier distortion due to flashlamp heating. By correcting each beam, a smaller spot\nsize can be achieved, producing higher power density or better-controlled focal spots at the target.\nWith the help of deformable mirrors, beams can reach the required spot size of about 100 microns or\nsmaller. That\u2019s less than the diameter of a human hair.\n\n### Adaptive Optics Improve Focus\n\nAdaptive optics are used in some telescopes to \u201cstraighten\u201d the wavefronts of light, thereby\nimproving the image resolution and contrast. Deformable mirrors have improved astronomical imaging\nfor land-based telescopes by a factor of 10 by compensating for atmospheric conditions.\n\nIn planning NIF, engineers knew that as the NIF beams made four passes through the amplifiers they\nwould accumulate wavefront aberrations due to distortions in the amplifier glass and other optics.\nThey needed to develop a way to compensate for these distortions to produce a well-controlled,\nfocused beam. The answer lay in the deformable mirror.\n\nAdvances in adaptive optics in the Atomic Vapor Laser Isotope Separation program at Lawrence\nLivermore led to the first small-size deformable mirror performance demonstration on the Beamlet\n(NIF prototype beam line) in 1994. A full-aperture (40-centimeter square) deformable mirror was\ninstalled on the Beamlet in early 1997.\n\nNext Up: [Rapid-Growth Crystals][176]\n\n[[LLNL]][177]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-642493\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][178]\n[[LLNS]][179] [[U.S. DOE]][180] [[NNSA]][181]\n[Home][182] [Disclaimer][183] [Privacy Notice][184]\n* [About][185]\n* [What Is NIF?][186]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][187]\n* [Laser Glass][188]\n* [Optical Switch][189]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][190]\n