rophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [How NIF Works][128]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][129]\n* [Power Conditioning System][130]\n* [Injection Laser System][131]\n* [Amplifiers][132]\n* [Beam Transport][133]\n* [Final Optics][134]\n* [Target Chamber][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [How NIF Works][159]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][160]\n* [Power Conditioning System][161]\n* [Injection Laser System][162]\n* [Amplifiers][163]\n* [Beam Transport][164]\n* [Final Optics][165]\n* [Target Chamber][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. How NIF Works\n\n# How NIF Works\n\nWhat happens when 192 of the world\u2019s highest-energy lasers converge on a target the size of a\npeppercorn filled with hydrogen atoms? Answer: the same thing that happens inside the Sun and the\nstars: fusion! NIF\u2019s laser beams can create nuclear fusion in the laboratory by generating the same\nkinds of temperatures and pressures that exist in the cores of stars and giant planets and inside\nnuclear weapons (see [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][175]).\n\nWe use NIF\u2019s lasers for several specific missions, including [Stockpile Stewardship][176], [high\nenergy density science][177], [Discovery Science, ][178][energy security][179], and [building future\ngenerations][180] of scientists. And we\u2019re researching more types of applications, including\n[advanced lasers and photonics][181], additive manufacturing, and missile defense.\n\n[Diagram of NIF Beamlines]\n\nAt the outset of a NIF experiment, a weak laser pulse\u2014about 1 billionth of a joule\u2014is created,\nsplit, and carried on optical fibers to 48 preamplifiers that increase the pulse\u2019s energy by a\nfactor of 10 billion, to a few joules. The 48 beams are then split into four beams each for\n[injection into the 192 main laser amplifier][182] beamlines.\n\nGuided by laser mirrors (LMs 1, 2, and 3), each beam zooms through two large glass\n[amplifiers][183], first through the power amplifier and then into the main amplifier. In the main\namplifier, a special [optical switch][184] called a plasma electrode Pockels cell (PEPC) traps the\nlight, forcing it to travel back and forth four times, while special deformable mirrors, spatial\nfilters, and other devices ensure the beams are high quality, uniform, and smooth (see [The Seven\nWonders of NIF][185]).\n\nFrom the main amplifier, the beam makes a final pass through the power amplifier. By now, the beams\u2019\ntotal energy has grown from 1 billionth of a joule to 4 million joules\u2014all in a few millionths of a\nsecond.\n\n[A NIF Preamplifier]\nNIF\u2019s preamplifier modules are unique and complex lasers in themselves. They take the\nnanojoule-level laser light from the master oscillator, amplifiy it more than a billion times, and\nset its spatial profile before injecting it into the main laser beampath.\n\nThe 192 beams proceed to two 10-story switchyards on either side of the Target Chamber where they\nare split into quads of 2\u00d72 arrays by a series of transport mirrors (see [Beam Transport][186]).\nJust before entering the [Target Chamber][187], each quad passes through a [final optics\nassembly][188], where the pulses are converted from 4 million joules infrared to 2 million joules\nultraviolet energy, and focused onto the target. NIF\u2019s 192 laser beams travel about 1,500 meters\nfrom their birth to their destination at the center of the spherical Target Chamber. Yet the journey\nfrom start to finish takes only about 5 microseconds.\n\n### How NIF\u2019s Lasers Work\n\n[Stimulated Emission of Light]\nEnergy from light excites electrons in atoms of optical materials and they move to a higher-energy\norbit. When the electrons return to their normal orbit spontaneously or when stimulated with light\nat exactly the right wavelength, they emit light particles called photons. Credit: James Wickboldt\n\nIn a sense NIF, the world\u2019s highest-energy laser system, is one big laser amplifier. As with most\nlarge lasers, NIF uses intense flashes of white light from giant flashlamps to \u201cpump\u201d electrons in\nlarge slabs of laser glass to a higher-energy state that lasts only about one-millionth of a second.\n\nA small pulse of laser light \u201ctuned\u201d to the excited electrons\u2019 energy is directed through the glass\nslabs. This laser pulse stimulates the electrons to drop to their lower, or ground, energy states\nand emit laser photons of exactly the same wavelength.\n\nThis process produces huge numbers of photons of the same wavelength and direction\u2014an extremely\nbright and straight beam of light. The initial low-energy pulse is amplified by more than a\nquadrillion times to create 192 highly energetic, tightly focused laser beams that converge in the\ncenter of the Target Chamber (see [How Lasers Work][189]).\n\n### How NIF Targets Work\n\n[A NIF Target]\nNIF targets are precisely controlled in a cryogenic cooling system that keeps the deuterium-tritium\nfuel frozen inside a millimeter-sized capsule.\n\nIn a NIF ignition experiment, a tiny capsule containing two forms of hydrogen, deuterium (D) and\ntritium (T), is suspended inside a cylindrical x-ray \u201coven\u201d called a hohlraum. When the hohlraum is\nheated by NIF\u2019s powerful lasers to temperatures of more than 3 million degrees Celsius, the\nresulting x rays heat and blow off, or ablate, the surface of the target capsule, called the\nablator. This causes a rocket-like implosion that compresses and heats the DT fuel to extreme\ntemperatures and densities until the hydrogen atoms fuse, creating helium nuclei (alpha particles)\nand releasing high-energy neutrons and other forms of energy.\n\nIf the implosion is symmetrical and compression and temperature in the \u201chot spot\u201d at the center of\nthe capsule are sufficient, the resulting alpha particles will spread through and heat the\nsurrounding cold fuel, triggering a self-sustaining fusion reaction. This process can generate\nenergy equalling or exceeding the energy delivered to the target, a condition known as ignition (see\n[Achieving Fusion Ignition][190]).\n\nNext Up: [Power Conditioning System][191]\n\n[[LLNL]][192]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-642493\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][193]\n[[LLNS]][194] [[U.S. DOE]][195] [[NNSA]][196]\n[Home][197] [Disclaimer][198] [Privacy Notice][199]\n* [About][200]\n* [What Is NIF?][201]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][202]\n* [Laser Glass][203]\n* [Optical Switch][204]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][205]\n* [Deformable Mirror][206]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][207]\n* [Computer Control System][208]\n* [Target Fabrication][209]\n* [How NIF Works][210]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][211]\n* [Power Conditioning System][212]\n* [Injection Laser System][213]\n* [Amplifiers][214]\n* [Beam Transport][215]\n* [Final Optics][216]\n* [Target Chamber][217]\n* [Keys to Our Success][218]\n* [Facility Operations][219]\n* [Power and Energy][220]\n* [Control Systems][221]\n* [Optics][222]\n* [Targets][223]\n* [Diagnostics][224]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][225]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][226]\n* [Key Steps on the Path to 