rophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [How NIF Works][128]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][129]\n* [Power Conditioning System][130]\n* [Injection Laser System][131]\n* [Amplifiers][132]\n* [Beam Transport][133]\n* [Final Optics][134]\n* [Target Chamber][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [How NIF Works][159]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][160]\n* [Power Conditioning System][161]\n* [Injection Laser System][162]\n* [Amplifiers][163]\n* [Beam Transport][164]\n* [Final Optics][165]\n* [Target Chamber][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. [How NIF Works][175]\n4. Injection Laser System\n\n# Injection Laser System\n\n[Master Oscillator Room]\nNIF\u2019s laser beams are born in the Master Oscillator Room. A compact laser oscillator generates\nlow-energy (a few nanojoules) laser pulses which are shaped in time and frequency-broadened to help\nsmooth the intensity of the laser beam when it is ultimately focused on the target.\n\nFor each of NIF\u2019s 192 beams:\n* The pulse shape as a function of time must be generated with a high degree of precision.\n* The energy delivered to the target must be precise.\n* The energy must be delivered to the target at exactly the specified time.\n\nNIF\u2019s injection laser system (ILS) plays a key role in meeting these three requirements. The ILS\nsystem is responsible for generating a prescribed temporal and spatial pulse shape, adjusting the\nenergy in each of the 192 beams, and adjusting the time when the beams hit the target. The ILS\nconsists of the Master Oscillator Room (MOR), the preamplifier module (PAM), the input sensor\npackage (ISP), and the preamplifier beam transport system (PABTS). NIF has one master oscillator, 48\nPAMs and ISPs, and 24 PABTSs.\n\n### Master Oscillator Room\n\n[The NIF Master Oscillator]\nThe NIF master oscillator.\n\nNIF laser pulses are generated in the MOR from an optical fiber laser. This laser generates pulses\nof just a few nanojoules (billionths of a joule) with a beam diameter of a few micrometers. There\nare four individual oscillators, one for each cone of beams on the target. Each oscillator\u2019s\nfrequency can be individually adjusted.\n\nMaster Oscillator Room equipment generates the low-energy laser pulses that are subsequently\namplified several billion times before being injected into the NIF laser amplifiers.\n\nThe pulse is then split and amplified using fiber splitters and fiber amplifiers similar to those\nused in the telecommunications industry. After several stages of splitting and amplification, 48\nindividual pulses are generated. Each pulse is then shaped in time and timed per the experimental\nrequirements and transported to each of the 48 PAMs for further amplification and spatial beam\nshaping.\n\n### Preamplifier Module\n\nThe PAM contains two different amplification stages. The first amplifies the pulse energy from about\n750 picojoules (trillionths of a joule) to about 15 millijoules (thousandths of a joule), a factor\nof 20 million. The second stage amplifies the shaped beam by a factor of up to 10,000. The output\nenergy of the PAM can be varied from about 10 millijoules to about 10 joules. In between the two\namplifiers the shape of the beam is converted from the typical round laser profile to a precise\nsquare-shaped beam to better fill the large laser amplifier aperture.\n\n### Input Sensor Package\n\nThe ISP performs three functions: aligning the PAM, generating a pilot beam to align the main laser\noptical system, and measuring the PAM output energy, beam profile, and pulse shape. Because there\nare no other energy adjustment devices in the main laser chain, the PAM energy must be precisely\nadjusted so that each beam on the ignition target has the requested energy. A fast photodiode\nmeasures the pulse shape in time to ensure that the pulses have the proper shape.\n\n### Preamplifier Beam Transport System\n\nThe beams produced by the two PAMs are each split into four beams in the PABTS system using a series\nof beam splitters (each beam is split in two and then the two beams are each split into two again).\nThe PABTS can adjust the split ratio\u2014the percentage of light into each of the four beams. In\naddition, the PABTS contains a series of mirrors for each beam that can be moved to change the\ndistance each beam propagates in the PABTS. This is used to make sure each beam of a quad arrives at\nprecisely the same time on the target. The timing of the quad is adjusted in the MOR.\n\nNext Up: [Amplifiers][176]\n\n[[LLNL]][177]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-737325\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][178]\n[[LLNS]][179] [[U.S. DOE]][180] [[NNSA]][181]\n[Home][182] [Disclaimer][183] [Privacy Notice][184]\n* [About][185]\n* [What Is NIF?][186]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][187]\n* [Laser Glass][188]\n* [Optical Switch][189]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][190]\n* [Deformable Mirror][191]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][192]\n* [Computer Control System][193]\n* [Target Fabrication][194]\n* [How NIF Works][195]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][196]\n* [Power Conditioning System][197]\n* [Injection Laser System][198]\n* [