            "text": "[ Skip to main content ][1]\n[U.S. flag]\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere\u2019s how you know\n\nHere\u2019s how you know\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\nA **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\nA **lock** ( [Lock] ) or **https://** means you\u2019ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share\nsensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n* [Our Purpose][2]\n* [Our Science and Technology][3]\n* [Join Our Team][4]\n* [Partner with Us][5]\n* [News][6]\n* [Community and Education][7]\n* [About][8]\n\n[[LLNL Logo]][9]\n[National Ignition Facility & Photon Science][10]\n\n## Primary Menu\n* [About ][11]\n* [What Is NIF?][12]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][13]\n* [Laser Glass][14]\n* [Optical Switch][15]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][16]\n* [Deformable Mirror][17]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][18]\n* [Computer Control System][19]\n* [Target Fabrication][20]\n* [How NIF Works][21]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][22]\n* [Power Conditioning System][23]\n* [Injection Laser System][24]\n* [Amplifiers][25]\n* [Beam Transport][26]\n* [Final Optics][27]\n* [Target Chamber][28]\n* [Keys to Our Success][29]\n* [Facility Operations][30]\n* [Power and Energy][31]\n* [Control Systems][32]\n* [Optics][33]\n* [Targets][34]\n* [Diagnostics][35]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][36]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][37]\n* [Who Works for NIF][38]\n* [People Profiles][39]\n* [Management][40]\n* [Developing the Workforce][41]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][42]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][43]\n* [Technology Transfer][44]\n* [Careers][45]\n* [FAQs][46]\n* [Visit Us][47]\n* [Science ][48]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][49]\n* [High Energy Density Science][50]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][51]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][52]\n* [National Security Applications][53]\n* [Developing New Capabilities][54]\n* [Discovery Science][55]\n* [Lab Astrophysics][56]\n* [Nuclear Astrophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [How NIF Works][128]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][129]\n* [Power Conditioning System][130]\n* [Injection Laser System][131]\n* [Amplifiers][132]\n* [Beam Transport][133]\n* [Final Optics][134]\n* [Target Chamber][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [How NIF Works][159]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][160]\n* [Power Conditioning System][161]\n* [Injection Laser System][162]\n* [Amplifiers][163]\n* [Beam Transport][164]\n* [Final Optics][165]\n* [Target Chamber][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. [How NIF Works][175]\n4. Amplifiers\n\n# Amplifiers\n\n[Amplifier Upper Plenums]\nNIF amplifier upper plenums, known as \u201cBigTs,\u201d are complex epoxy manifolds that distribute both\npulsed power to the flashlamps and cooling/purge gas to the amplifier and flashlamp cavities.\n\nFrom the injection laser system, the laser beams enter the main laser amplifier beamlines in groups\nof four called quads; two quads make up a bundle, and six bundles constitute a cluster. Four\nclusters\u2014two in each of the two laser bays\u2014add up to NIF\u2019s total of 192 laser beams. Each beamline\ncontains two large amplifier sections, the main amplifier and the power amplifier. The amplifiers\nare designed to efficiently provide all of NIF\u2019s power and energy.\n\n[NIF Amplifier Slabs]\nNIF amplifier slabs are set at Brewster\u2019s angle to minimize reflective losses.\n\nThe amplifiers vastly augment the weak 1-joule input pulse sent from the injection laser system and\ndetermine the beam\u2019s spatial, spectral, and temporal characteristics. When fully integrated with\nlaser amplifier glass, flashlamps, pulsed power, and other related components, the amplifiers are\nthe largest of NIF\u2019s integrated systems, occupying approximately one-fourth of the facility\u2019s floor\nspace.\n\n### Laser Glass\n\nNIF\u2019s amplifiers use 3,072 42-kilogram neodymium-doped phosphate glass slabs\u201416 glass slabs per\nbeam. Each measures 3.4 by 46 by 81 centimeters and is set on edge at a specific angle, known as\nBrewster\u2019s angle, so that the laser beams have very low reflective losses while passing through the\nglass. The glass slabs are \u201cdoped\u201d with neodymium atoms, which impart a pinkish color to the glass.\n\nWhen the slabs are bathed in the intense white light of the flashlamps that surround them, the\nneodymium atoms are raised to higher energy states (see [How Lasers Work][176]). When a low-energy\nlaser pulse from the injection laser system passes through the slabs, the neodymium atoms release\ntheir extra energy into the laser pulse in the form of photons, increasing the pulse\u2019s brightness.\nThe neodymium-doped phosphate laser glass is the result of a six-year joint research and development\nprogram with industrial partners SCHOTT North America and Hoya Corp. USA. This effort developed a\n[revolutionary continuous pour process][177] for manufacturing meter-size slabs of laser glass that\nis 10 times faster, 5 times cheaper, and with better optical quality\n\n### Flashlamps\n\nThe amplifier slabs are surrounded by vertical arrays of flashlamps. Measuring nearly 180\ncentimeters (6 feet) tall, NIF\u2019s 7,680 flashlamps are the largest commercial lamps ever made. When\ndriven with about 50,000 joules of electrical energy, the flashlamps emit bright white light that is\nabsorbed by the neodymium in the glass slabs, exciting the atoms to provide optical gain at the\n1,053-nanometer (infrared) wavelength, also referred to as 1\u03c9 or \u201c1 omega\u201d light. The flashlamps are\nforced-air cooled between shots to prevent the amplifier slabs from heating up and reduce resulting\nbeam distortions in order to meet the operational requirement of one shot every eight hours.\n\n### Automated Guided Vehicles\n\n[A NIF Laser Bay Transporter]\nThe laser bay transporter, an automated guided vehicle, is used to install canisters containing\namplifier slab cassettes, known as line replaceable units, into the main amplifier frame assembly\nunits.\n\nAssembled and tested amplifier and flashlamp modules are loaded into Class 100 clean-room\nenvironment canisters before being transported to the laser bays. The canisters are mounted on\nspecial automated guide vehicles and moved to the beampath. The transporters position the canisters,\ndocking them to the amplifier structure to a tolerance of 1 millimeter and one-tenth of a degree\nrotation. The computer-controlled transporters use wall-mounted fiducials, or reference points, to\nprecisely navigate the facility and negotiate tight corners to their destination with only\ncentimeters of clearance.\n\nNext Up: [Beam Transport][178]\n\n[[LLNL]][179]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-642493\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][180]\n[[LLNS]][181] [[U.S. DOE]][182] [[NNSA]][183]\n[Home][184] [Disclaimer][185] [Privacy Notice][186]\n* [About][187]\n* [What Is NIF?][188]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][189]\n* [Laser Glass][190]\n* [Optical Switch][191]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][192]\n* [Deformable Mirror][193]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals]