rophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [How NIF Works][128]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][129]\n* [Power Conditioning System][130]\n* [Injection Laser System][131]\n* [Amplifiers][132]\n* [Beam Transport][133]\n* [Final Optics][134]\n* [Target Chamber][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [How NIF Works][159]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][160]\n* [Power Conditioning System][161]\n* [Injection Laser System][162]\n* [Amplifiers][163]\n* [Beam Transport][164]\n* [Final Optics][165]\n* [Target Chamber][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. [How NIF Works][175]\n4. Beam Transport\n\n# Beam Transport\n\n[NIF Laser Bay]\nEach NIF laser bay is 122 meters (400 feet) long and contains 96 beamlines. This side view of Laser\nBay 2 shows the four-high laser transport beamline enclosures above the preamplifier support\nstructure.\n\nAs NIF\u2019s laser beams are propagated four times through the main amplifier and twice through the\npower amplifier, their energy is boosted to about 4 megajoules of infrared laser light.\n\nWithin the laser beam enclosures are two stages of spatial filters that condition the beams after\namplification. Before being transported to the Target Chamber, each beam is focused through a series\nof tiny, millimeter-sized pinholes and recollimated in NIF\u2019s spatial filters. These pinholes are\nlocated at the center of the huge beam tubes that create a vacuum environment so the laser beams can\nbe concentrated into such a small spot size without ionizing the air molecules.\n\n[NIF Deformable Mirror]\n[Deformable mirrors][176], located at the ends of NIF\u2019s main amplifiers, use an array of 39\nactuators to create a movable surface that corrects aberrations in a beam due to minute distortions\nin the optics.\n\nPassing the beams through the pinholes eliminates scattered laser light, creating smooth,\nhigh-quality beams capable of meeting stringent performance requirements at high power and energy.\n\n### Switchyards\n\nAfter the laser beams leave the laser bays, they enter the switchyards, which redirect the beams to\nthe upper and lower hemispheres of the [Target Chamber][177]. The switchyards convert the parallel\nlaser beam layout to the spherical configuration of the Target Chamber so they converge radially on\nthe target.\n\nThe beams travel through the switchyards in groups of four called \u201cquads.\u201d With the help of several\nseries of transport mirrors, 24 beam quads are redirected to the upper portion of the Target Chamber\nand 24 are redirected to the bottom portion. Four mirrors are required to accomplish this, along\nwith a fifth mirror for one-third of the beams because of geometric constraints. The mirrors are\nspecially coated and conditioned to survive NIF\u2019s high-energy laser environment. They are also\ndesigned to be extremely stable in the vibration and thermal environment of the huge NIF facility so\nthat the laser beams can hit the target with a precision of better than 50 micrometers\u2014about the\nthickness of a piece of paper.\n\nNext Up: [Final Optics][178]\n\n[[LLNL]][179]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-642493\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][180]\n[[LLNS]][181] [[U.S. DOE]][182] [[NNSA]][183]\n[Home][184] [Disclaimer][185] [Privacy Notice][186]\n* [About][187]\n* [What Is NIF?][188]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][189]\n* [Laser Glass][190]\n* [Optical Switch][191]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][192]\n* [Deformable Mirror][193]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][194]\n* [Computer Control System][195]\n* [Target Fabrication][196]\n* [How NIF Works][197]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][198]\n* [Power Conditioning System][199]\n* [Injection Laser System][200]\n* [Amplifiers][201]\n* [Beam Transport][202]\n* [Final Optics][203]\n* [Target Chamber][204]\n* [Keys to Our Success][205]\n* [Facility Operations][206]\n* [Power and Energy][207]\n* [Control Systems][208]\n* [Optics][209]\n* [Targets][210]\n* [Diagnostics][211]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][212]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][213]\n* [Key Steps on the Path to NIF][214]\n* [50 Years of Laser Leadership][215]\n* [Building NIF][216]\n* [Who Works for NIF][217]\n* [People Profiles][218]\n* [Management][219]\n* [Developing the Workforce][220]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][221]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][222]\n* [Technology Transfer][223]\n* [Careers][224]\n* [FAQs][225]\n* [Visit Us][226]\n* [Science][227]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][228]\n* [High Energy Density Science][229]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][230]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][231]\n* [National Security Applications][232]\n* [Developing New Capabilities][233]\n* [Discovery Science][234]\n* [Lab Astrophysics][235]\n* [Nuclear Astrophysics][236]\n* [Planetary Physics][237]\n* [Plasma Physics][238]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][239]\n* [DE Technology Programs][240]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][241]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][242]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][243]\n* [Fiber Lasers][244]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][245]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][246]\n* [Journal Articles][247]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][248]\n* [NIF U