            "text": "[ Skip to main content ][1]\n[U.S. flag]\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere\u2019s how you know\n\nHere\u2019s how you know\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\nA **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\nA **lock** ( [Lock] ) or **https://** means you\u2019ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share\nsensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n* [Our Purpose][2]\n* [Our Science and Technology][3]\n* [Join Our Team][4]\n* [Partner with Us][5]\n* [News][6]\n* [Community and Education][7]\n* [About][8]\n\n[[LLNL Logo]][9]\n[National Ignition Facility & Photon Science][10]\n\n## Primary Menu\n* [About ][11]\n* [What Is NIF?][12]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][13]\n* [Laser Glass][14]\n* [Optical Switch][15]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][16]\n* [Deformable Mirror][17]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][18]\n* [Computer Control System][19]\n* [Target Fabrication][20]\n* [How NIF Works][21]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][22]\n* [Power Conditioning System][23]\n* [Injection Laser System][24]\n* [Amplifiers][25]\n* [Beam Transport][26]\n* [Final Optics][27]\n* [Target Chamber][28]\n* [Keys to Our Success][29]\n* [Facility Operations][30]\n* [Power and Energy][31]\n* [Control Systems][32]\n* [Optics][33]\n* [Targets][34]\n* [Diagnostics][35]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][36]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][37]\n* [Who Works for NIF][38]\n* [People Profiles][39]\n* [Management][40]\n* [Developing the Workforce][41]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][42]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][43]\n* [Technology Transfer][44]\n* [Careers][45]\n* [FAQs][46]\n* [Visit Us][47]\n* [Science ][48]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][49]\n* [High Energy Density Science][50]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][51]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][52]\n* [National Security Applications][53]\n* [Developing New Capabilities][54]\n* [Discovery Science][55]\n* [Lab Astrophysics][56]\n* [Nuclear Astrophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [How NIF Works][128]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][129]\n* [Power Conditioning System][130]\n* [Injection Laser System][131]\n* [Amplifiers][132]\n* [Beam Transport][133]\n* [Final Optics][134]\n* [Target Chamber][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [How NIF Works][159]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][160]\n* [Power Conditioning System][161]\n* [Injection Laser System][162]\n* [Amplifiers][163]\n* [Beam Transport][164]\n* [Final Optics][165]\n* [Target Chamber][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. [How NIF Works][175]\n4. Final Optics\n\n# Final Optics\n\n[NIF Final Optics Assemblies]\nFinal optics assemblies attached to the lower hemisphere of the NIF Target Chamber.\n\nThe final optics assemblies (FOAs) are the last element of the main laser system and the first of\nthe target area systems. Each FOA contains four integrated optics modules (IOMs) that incorporate\nbeam conditioning, frequency conversion, focusing, diagnostic sampling, and debris shielding\ncapabilities into a single compact assembly. These optics are shown in the figure below.\n\n[Final Optics Assembly Schematic]\nSchematic layout of NIF\u2019s final optics assembly. The suite of optics for one beamline is on the\nright.\n\nThe FOAs mount to the NIF Target Chamber and house the final set of optics for four NIF beamlines as\nline replaceable units; each optic can be individually replaced as required. New methods of\nfabricating, grinding, and polishing were required to produce [optics that could\nwithstand][176] NIF\u2019s extremes of energy and power.\n\nNIF\u2019s 192 beamlines connect to the Target Chamber in groups of four through the 48 FOAs. The FOAs\nare symmetrically distributed around the upper and lower hemispheres of the Target Chamber so that\nthe laser beams are optimally located to provide the proper orientation as they are directed toward\nthe target. Adjustments of the final turning mirrors allow pointing the focused beams to different\nlocations near Target Chamber Center (\u00b1 3 centimeters) to accommodate a variety of experiments.\n\nOne of the chief functions of the FOAs is to change the laser light to a shorter wavelength. NIF\u2019s\nneodymium glass lasers generate light at a fundamental wavelength of about 1,053 nanometers (one\nomega, or 1\u03c9) in the infrared region. [Inertial confinement fusion][177] targets, however, perform\nmore efficiently when they are driven with 351-nm ultraviolet light (3\u03c9).\n\nTo change the laser light frequency, the 1\u03c9 light passes through two crystal plates made of\npotassium dihydrogen phosphate (KDP), the same type of crystal used in NIF\u2019s [Pockels cell optical\nswitch][178] (this process is based on the nonlinear optical response of KDP to high-power laser\nlight). The first plate converts two-thirds of the 1,053-nanometer radiation to the 527-nm second\nharmonic (2\u03c9) wavelength, which is visible green light. The second crystal then mixes that radiation\nwith the remaining infrared light to produce 351-nm ultraviolet radiation at the third harmonic\n(3\u03c9).\n\nOther functions of the FOAs are also critical to achieving the required laser performance for use in\ntarget experiments. In all, the FOAs provide a vacuum barrier for the Target Chamber, convert 1\u03c9 to\n3\u03c9 light, focus the 3\u03c9 light to Target Chamber center in a shape consistent with the experimental\nrequirements, allow for beam smoothing, allow for 3\u03c9 power and energy measurement, and provide a\nprotective shield from target debris.\n\nReplacement of each final optic is done using specialized clean cassettes that can be attached to\nthe IOM hatches.\n\n### More Information\n\n[\u201cNIF final optics system: frequency conversion and beam conditioning\u201d][179]\n\nNext Up: [Target Chamber][180]\n\n[[LLNL]][181]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-642493\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][182]\n[[LLNS]][183] [[U.S. DOE]][184] [[NNSA]][185]\n[Home][186] [Disclaimer][187] [Privacy Notice][188]\n* [About][189]\n* [What Is NIF?][190]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][191]\n* [Laser Glass][192]\n* [Optical Switch][193]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][194]\n* [Deformable Mirror][195]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][196]\n* [Computer Control System][197]\n* [Target Fabrication][198]\n* [How NIF Works][199]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][200]\n* [Power Conditioning System][201]\n* [Injection Laser System][202]\n* [Amplifiers][203]\n* [Beam Transport][204]\n* [Final Optics][205]\n* [Target Chamber][206]\n* [Keys to Our Success][207]\n* [Facility Operations][208]\n* [Power and Energy][209]\n* [Control Systems][210]\n* [Optics][211]\n* [Targets][212]\n* [Diagnostics][213]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][214]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][215]\n* [Key Steps on the Path to NIF][216]\n* [50 Years of Laser Leadership][217]\n* [Building NIF][218]\n* [Who Works for NIF][219]\n* [People Profiles][220]\n* [Management][221]\n* [Developing the Workforce][222]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][223]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][224]\n* [Technology Transfer][225]\n* [Careers][226]\n* [FAQs][227]\n* [Visit Us][228]\n* [Science][229]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][230]\n* [High Energy Density Science][231]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][232]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][233]\n* [National Security Applications][234]\n* [Developing New Capabilities][235]\n* [Discovery Science][236]\n* [Lab Astrophysics][237]\n* [Nuclear Astrophysics][238]\n* [Planetary Physics][239]\n* [Plasma Physics][240]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][241]\n* [DE Technology Programs][242]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][243]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][244]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][245]\n* [Fiber Lasers][246]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][247]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][248]\n* [Journal Articles][249]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][250]\n* [NIF Users][251]\n* [NIF User Portal][252]\n* [NIF User Guide][253]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][254]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][255]\n* [Shot RI Resources][256]\n* [Call for Proposals][257]\n* [Prior Year Awards][258]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][259]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][260]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][261]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][262]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][263]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][264]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][265]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][266]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][267]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][268]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][269]\n* [NIF User Group][270]\n* [Bylaws][271]\n* [Executive Board][272]\n* [NIF Calendar][273]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][274]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][275]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][276]\n* [Multimedia][277]\n* [Photo Gallery][278]\n* [Playing Cards][279]\n* [Poster Gallery][280]\n* [Video Gallery][281]\n* [Education][282]\n* [How Lasers Work][283]\n* [How to Make a Star][284]\n* [Outreach][285]\n* [Laser Roadshow][286]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][287]\n* [Seminars][288]\n* [Student Spotlight][289]\n* [Scholar Tours][290]\n* [Glossary][291]\n* [External Resources][292]\n* [News][293]\n* [The Age of Ignition][294]\n* [In the News][295]