rophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [How NIF Works][128]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][129]\n* [Power Conditioning System][130]\n* [Injection Laser System][131]\n* [Amplifiers][132]\n* [Beam Transport][133]\n* [Final Optics][134]\n* [Target Chamber][135]\n* [Keys to Our Success][136]\n* [Who Works for NIF][137]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][138]\n* [Careers][139]\n* [FAQs][140]\n* [Visit Us][141]\n* [Science][142]\n* [NIF Users][143]\n* [Multimedia][144]\n* [Education][145]\n* [News][146]\n* [Programs][147]\n* [Contact][148]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][149]\n* [Research][150]\n* [Doing Business][151]\n* [News][152]\n* [About][153]\n* [Careers][154]\n* [Community][155]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][156]\n* [What Is NIF?][157]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][158]\n* [How NIF Works][159]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][160]\n* [Power Conditioning System][161]\n* [Injection Laser System][162]\n* [Amplifiers][163]\n* [Beam Transport][164]\n* [Final Optics][165]\n* [Target Chamber][166]\n* [Keys to Our Success][167]\n* [Who Works for NIF][168]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][169]\n* [Careers][170]\n* [FAQs][171]\n* [Visit Us][172]\n1. [Home][173]\n2. [About][174]\n3. [How NIF Works][175]\n4. Target Chamber\n\n# Target Chamber\n\n[The NIF Target Chamber]\nTarget and diagnostic positioners deployed at the center of the NIF Target Chamber.\n\nThe \"Grand Central Station\" of the National Ignition Facility is the Target Bay, which houses the\nfinal optics assemblies, diagnostics, and the Target Chamber.\n\nPulses from NIF\u2019s 192 high-powered lasers, in a journey that originates more than 100 meters away in\nthe Master Oscillator Room, race toward the Target Bay with incredible precision. When they arrive\nat the center of the Target Chamber, they deposit as much as 500 trillion watts of power for up to\n20 billionths of a second on an eraser-sized capsule containing a [target the size of a BB][176].\n\n[The NIF Target Chamber Composite]\nComposite photograph showing three stories of the Target Bay and many of the final optics assemblies\nand diagnostics surrounding the Target Chamber at the center. Credit: Jacqueline McBride\n\nSurrounding the target are [many sensitive diagnostics][177] that examine in minute detail the\narrival of the laser beams and the reaction of the target to this sudden deposition of energy.\nTemperatures of 100 million degrees and pressures extreme enough to compress the target to densities\nmany times the density of lead are created.\n\nThe Target Bay is a concrete silo 30 meters high and 30 meters in diameter and is filled with laser,\ndiagnostic, and other utility/support equipment needed to conduct experiments safely and reliably.\n\nThe Target Chamber with its utility systems provides the vacuum environment needed during the\nexperiment, holds the final optics assemblies that focus the laser light on the target, and contains\ndebris and x rays generated during experiments. The chamber, a sphere 10 meters in diameter, was\nassembled from 10-centimeter-thick aluminum panels that were pre-formed and then welded in place. It\nis covered with 0.3 meters of concrete that has been injected with boron to absorb neutrons from\nfusion reactions. The remaining neutrons are absorbed by the concrete Target Bay and switchyard\nwalls to protect workers and the public.\n\nThe Target Chamber is filled with holes that permit the laser beams to enter the chamber and to\nprovide viewing ports for all of the diagnostics. The diagnostic ports are about one-half meter in\ndiameter and are generally concentrated around the \"waist,\" or equator, of the Target Chamber\n\nTarget handling systems precisely position the target at Target Chamber center and freeze the target\nto cryogenic temperatures so that the fusion reactions can be more easily achieved. Seven-meter-long\npositioners are located just outside the Target Chamber. They can be retracted to permit\ninstallation of a target and then extended into position at Target Chamber center. Most targets are\nmaintained at very cold temperatures, about 18 kelvins (-427 degrees Fahrenheit). One of the\npositioners can maintain this temperature precisely to a tolerance of better than 0.001 degrees\ncentigrade.\n\n[[LLNL]][178]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-642493\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][179]\n[[LLNS]][180] [[U.S. DOE]][181] [[NNSA]][182]\n[Home][183] [Disclaimer][184] [Privacy Notice][185]\n* [About][186]\n* [What Is NIF?][187]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][188]\n* [Laser Glass][189]\n* [Optical Switch][190]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][191]\n* [Deformable Mirror][192]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][193]\n* [Computer Control System][194]\n* [Target Fabrication][195]\n* [How NIF Works][196]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][197]\n* [Power Conditioning System][198]\n* [Injection Laser System][199]\n* [Amplifiers][200]\n* [Beam Transport][201]\n* [Final Optics][202]\n* [Target Chamber][203]\n* [Keys to Our Success][204]\n* [Facility Operations][205]\n* [Power and Energy][206]\n* [Control Systems][207]\n* [Optics][208]\n* [Targets][209]\n* [Diagnostics][210]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][211]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][212]\n* [Key Steps on the Path to NIF][213]\n* [50 Years of Laser Leadership][214]\n* [Building NIF][215]\n* [Who Works for NIF][216]\n* [People Profiles][217]\n* [Management][218]\n* [Developing the Workforce][219]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][220]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][221]\n* [Technology Transfer][222]\n* [Careers][223]\n* [FAQs][224]\n* [Visit Us][225]\n* [Science][226]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][227]\n* [High Energy Density Science][228]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][229]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][230]\n* [National Security Applications][231]\n* [Developing