rophysics][57]\n* [Planetary Physics][58]\n* [Plasma Physics][59]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][60]\n* [DE Technology Programs][61]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][62]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][63]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][64]\n* [Fiber Lasers][65]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][66]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][67]\n* [Journal Articles][68]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][69]\n* [NIF Users ][70]\n* [NIF User Portal][71]\n* [NIF User Guide][72]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][73]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][74]\n* [Shot RI Resources][75]\n* [Call for Proposals][76]\n* [Prior Year Awards][77]\n* [2024 Awarded Campaigns][78]\n* [2023 Awarded Campaigns][79]\n* [2022 Awarded Campaigns][80]\n* [2021 Awarded Campaigns][81]\n* [2020 Awarded Campaigns][82]\n* [2019 Awarded Campaigns][83]\n* [2018 Awarded Campaigns][84]\n* [2017 Awarded Campaigns][85]\n* [2016 Awarded Campaigns][86]\n* [2015 Awarded Campaigns][87]\n* [2014 Awarded Campaigns][88]\n* [NIF User Group][89]\n* [Bylaws][90]\n* [Executive Board][91]\n* [NIF Calendar][92]\n* [Target Shot Metrics][93]\n* [Conflict of Interest Policy][94]\n* [Jupiter Laser Facility][95]\n* [Multimedia ][96]\n* [Photo Gallery][97]\n* [Playing Cards][98]\n* [Poster Gallery][99]\n* [Video Gallery][100]\n* [Education ][101]\n* [How Lasers Work][102]\n* [How to Make a Star][103]\n* [Outreach][104]\n* [Laser Roadshow][105]\n* [Summer Scholar Internship Program][106]\n* [Seminars][107]\n* [Student Spotlight][108]\n* [Scholar Tours][109]\n* [Glossary][110]\n* [External Resources][111]\n* [News ][112]\n* [The Age of Ignition][113]\n* [In the News][114]\n* [NIF in the News: Video][115]\n* [Programs ][116]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][117]\n* [Facilities][118]\n* [Publications][119]\n* [People][120]\n* [Job Postings][121]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][122]\n* [Directed Energy Technology Programs][123]\n* [Contact][124]\n\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\nSearch\n\n## Mobile Menu\n\n##### Site Menu\n* [About][125]\n* [What Is NIF?][126]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][127]\n* [How NIF Works][128]\n* [Keys to Our Success][129]\n* [Who Works for NIF][130]\n* [People Profiles][131]\n* [Management][132]\n* [Developing the Workforce][133]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][134]\n* [Careers][135]\n* [FAQs][136]\n* [Visit Us][137]\n* [Science][138]\n* [NIF Users][139]\n* [Multimedia][140]\n* [Education][141]\n* [News][142]\n* [Programs][143]\n* [Contact][144]\n\n##### LLNL Menu\n* [Missions][145]\n* [Research][146]\n* [Doing Business][147]\n* [News][148]\n* [About][149]\n* [Careers][150]\n* [Community][151]\n\n## Secondary Menu\n\n[About][152]\n* [What Is NIF?][153]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][154]\n* [How NIF Works][155]\n* [Keys to Our Success][156]\n* [Who Works for NIF][157]\n* [People Profiles][158]\n* [Management][159]\n* [Developing the Workforce][160]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][161]\n* [Careers][162]\n* [FAQs][163]\n* [Visit Us][164]\n1. [Home][165]\n2. [About][166]\n3. [Who Works for NIF & PS?][167]\n4. Developing the Workforce\n\n# Developing the Workforce\n\n[Researchers Receive PECASE Award]\n\n# Training Future Science Stars\n\n*LLNL researchers Richard Krause (left) of the Physical and Life Sciences Directorate and Arthur\nPak, Daniel Casey, and F\u00e9licie Albert of NIF & Photon Science were honored with the prestigious\nPresidential Early Career Award for Scientists and Engineers (PECASE) in 2019. Credit: Julie\nRussell*\n[Join Our Team][168]\n\nFor NIF & Photon Science, stewardship means attracting, training, and retaining future generations\nof scientists, engineers, programmers, technicians, and other skilled workers by constantly\nchallenging them to solve hard problems, so they\u2019re always ready to support the Laboratory\u2019s\nimportant missions.\n\n> \u201cThe training of scientists through the study of HED physics is an excellent contribution to the\n> STEM (science, technology, engineering, and math) workforce. These individuals gain fundamental,\n> interdisciplinary knowledge of extreme states of matter using the most advanced technical systems\n> that exist.\u201d\n> \u2014[Plasma Science Decadal Report][169]\n\nFor example, NIF experiments help maintain the skills of nuclear weapons scientists and train the\nnext generation of experts to maintain the nation\u2019s nuclear stockpile (see [NIF and Stockpile\nStewardship][170]).\n\nFrom facility tours, to our highly competitive summer student program, to our postdoctoral scholar\nappointments, NIF is training the future science stars who will continue the nation\u2019s leadership in\nscience and technology.\n\nAn especially valuable source of next-generation stewardship scientists is through NIF\u2019s\ncollaborations with universities in its [Discovery Science][171] (DS) program. Making NIF available\nto outside researchers not only provides opportunities for advancing basic science; it also serves\nas career development and an opportunity to engage in innovative [high energy density][172] (HED)\nscience for NIF scientists, who spend most of their time performing experiments in pursuit of\nlong-range national security goals.\n\n[Team Members Plan Experiment]\nMembers of the \u201cGbar\u201d (gigabar, or one million Earth atmospheres) Discovery Science team plan their\nexperiments to measure the equation of state (EOS) at extreme pressures by using streaked\nradiography of spherical implosions of solid plastic spheres (see \u201c[Record EOS Measurements Shed\nLight on Stellar Evolution][173]\u201d. The Gbar Campaign was initiated by Roger Falcone and his students\nand postdocs at UC Berkeley, other NIF academic users, and early career scientists from LLNL. From\nleft: Benjamin Bachmann, Tilo D\u00f6ppner, Joe Nilsen, and Annie Kritcher of LLNL and Alison Saunders\nand Falcone of UC Berkeley. Like many other students who have worked on NIF & Photon Science\nprojects, Saunders has since joined LLNL as a staff scientist. Credit: Jason Laurea\n\nBy devoting a nominal portion of their time to DS projects, NIF scientists stay current on\ncutting-edge science and experimental techniques, which in turn keeps their thinking nimble and\nleads to publication in high-impact journals.\n\nIn addition, NIF\u2019s academic users are innovative thinkers and future leaders in the scientific\ncommunity\u2014just the sort of people with whom LLNL would wish to form lasting relationships.\n\n[Members of the Nucleosynthesis Team]\nMembers of a Discovery Science team studying [stellar and Big Bang nucleosynthesis][174] via\nexperiments on NIF (from left): Charles Yeamans (LLNL), Daniel Sayre (LLNL), Matthias Hohenberger\n(Laboratory for Laser Energetics, University of Rochester), Maria Gatu Johnson (MIT), Daniel Casey\n(LLNL), Alex Zylstra (Los Alamos National Laboratory), Bruce Remington (LLNL), and Hong Sio (MIT).\nIndiana University and Ohio University also are participating in the campaign.\n\nWhile encouraging potential new hires to pursue careers at the Laboratory, LLNL also benefits from\nits ability to offer continued access to leading-edge research. All LLNL scientists who assist\nDiscovery Science users do so voluntarily.\n\n[Join Our Team][175]\n\nIn the decade since the inception of the fundamental science program, more than a dozen scientists\nwho brought projects to the program while in graduate school have joined Lawrence Livermore, Los\nAlamos, and Sandia national laboratories\u2014clear evidence that the program provides a success path\nbetween top academic institutions and the labs.\n\n[[LLNL]][176]\nLawrence Livermore National Laboratory\n| 7000 East Avenue \u2022 Livermore, CA 94550 | LLNL-WEB-817329\nOperated by the Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC for the Department of Energy's National\nNuclear Security Administration Learn about the Department of Energy's [Vulnerability Disclosure\nProgram][177]\n[[LLNS]][178] [[U.S. DOE]][179] [[NNSA]][180]\n[Home][181] [Disclaimer][182] [Privacy Notice][183]\n* [About][184]\n* [What Is NIF?][185]\n* [The Seven Wonders of NIF][186]\n* [Laser Glass][187]\n* [Optical Switch][188]\n* [Preamplifier Modules][189]\n* [Deformable Mirror][190]\n* [Rapid-Growth Crystals][191]\n* [Computer Control System][192]\n* [Target Fabrication][193]\n* [How NIF Works][194]\n* [Anatomy of a NIF Shot][195]\n* [Power Conditioning System][196]\n* [Injection Laser System][197]\n* [Amplifiers][198]\n* [Beam Transport][199]\n* [Final Optics][200]\n* [Target Chamber][201]\n* [Keys to Our Success][202]\n* [Facility Operations][203]\n* [Power and Energy][204]\n* [Control Systems][205]\n* [Optics][206]\n* [Targets][207]\n* [Diagnostics][208]\n* [Efficiency Improvements][209]\n* [A Legacy of Lasers][210]\n* [Key Steps on the Path to NIF][211]\n* [50 Years of Laser Leadership][212]\n* [Building NIF][213]\n* [Who Works for NIF][214]\n* [People Profiles][215]\n* [Management][216]\n* [Developing the Workforce][217]\n* [Who Partners With NIF][218]\n* [Enduring Partnerships][219]\n* [Technology Transfer][220]\n* [Careers][221]\n* [FAQs][222]\n* [Visit Us][223]\n* [Science][224]\n* [NIF and Stockpile Modernization][225]\n* [High Energy Density Science][226]\n* [Advancing Weapon Physics][227]\n* [Achieving Fusion Ignition][228]\n* [National Security Applications][229]\n* [Developing New Capabilities][230]\n* [Discovery Science][231]\n* [Lab Astrophysics][232]\n* [Nuclear Astrophysics][233]\n* [Planetary Physics][234]\n* [Plasma Physics][235]\n* [Expanding Photonics Frontiers][236]\n* [DE Technology Programs][237]\n* [Advanced Optical Technologies][238]\n* [Advanced Photon Technologies][239]\n* [Advanced Laser Systems][240]\n* [Fiber Lasers][241]\n* [Materials for Laser Systems][242]\n* [Exploring Energy Security][243]\n* [Journal Articles][244]\n* [Livermore Inertial Fusion Energy References][245]\n* [NIF Users][246]\n* [NIF User Portal][247]\n* [NIF User Guide][248]\n* [NIF DLI User Guide][249]\n* [Experimental Capabilities][250]\n* [Shot RI Resources][251]\n* [Call for Proposals